.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_USED_COUNT 3 "2026-09-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_used_count \- report how many pool elements are in use
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

size_t ccol_mempool_used_count(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the number of pool-owned elements that callers hold at the
moment. An element sitting in a thread's cache has left the shared free
list, but the pool gave it to nobody, so it doesn't count as used. A
dynamic fallback entry isn't counted either;
ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count(3) reports those.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp                        Memory pool to query (must not be NULL)
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns the number of elements in use at the moment
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The count is exact whenever the pool is quiet. If other threads
allocate or free while the call runs, the result is a snapshot that
can be stale by the time the call returns, as any count of something
that keeps moving is.

On a quiet pool the result never goes above
ccol_mempool_total_capacity(). Under concurrent allocation on a pool
with a cache for each thread it can be a few entries above that for a
short time, but never above the physical slot count that
ccol_mempool_allocated_bytes(3) reports. ccol_mempool_total_capacity(3)
explains why.

A NULL mp is a fatal error: the call prints a message and ends the
process with abort(), in every build.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe when the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
The call takes the pool's lock and walks the cache of each thread, so
it belongs on a cold path; don't use it inside a hot loop.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_total_capacity (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_dynamic_allocs_count (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
